H-CERNY(a): consec = 'fastest synchronizer'? first-cover-time of the
            full prefix vs. coverage by sparse subsets
==========================================================================
   span-domination measS7(E) <= measS7(consec_{max(E)+1}): tested 957, violations = 0
   => PROVED-style fact (this run): the FULL PREFIX 0..W covers for
      the WIDEST x-measure among all subsets of {0..W}; equality iff
      E already contains a covering sub-collection for a.e. covered x.

==========================================================================
H-CERNY(b): 'reset cost' = span the prefix needs to match a sparse E's
            cover; does consec win at EQUAL CARDINALITY by paying less?
==========================================================================
   first-cover-time CDF  F(t)=measS7(consec_{t+1}) (consec needs t+1 indices):
     t= 6: F(t)=measS7(consec_7)=0.147619  increment=0.147619
     t= 7: F(t)=measS7(consec_8)=0.327211  increment=0.179592
     t= 8: F(t)=measS7(consec_9)=0.416156  increment=0.088946
     t= 9: F(t)=measS7(consec_10)=0.504478  increment=0.088322
     t=10: F(t)=measS7(consec_11)=0.581463  increment=0.076984
     t=11: F(t)=measS7(consec_12)=0.624114  increment=0.042651
     t=12: F(t)=measS7(consec_13)=0.675928  increment=0.051814
     t=13: F(t)=measS7(consec_14)=0.704745  increment=0.028818
   For a sparse E with cardinality k and span W>k: it 'spreads' k probes
   over a window of W+1 slots. Cover prob <= F(W) but it uses only k slots.
   consec_k uses k slots over window k-1: cover prob = F(k-1).
   CRUX restated: is F(k-1) [pay k, span k] >= best sparse cover [pay k, span>k]?
   This is the genuine reset/Cerny TRADE: SPAN buys coverage, CARDINALITY
   is the budget. consec spends its whole budget on the SHORTEST covering
   window. The open inequality = 'cheapest reset wins'.

